WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.085 --> 00:00:08.584
A new Mars rover rolls across red sand in the Atacama Desert in Chile.

2
00:00:08.585 --> 00:00:15.763
The rover has all of the latest technology developed by NASA to aid in the study of Mars.

3
00:00:15.764 --> 00:00:20.255
Why are NASA scientists testing a Mars rover in South America?

4
00:00:20.256 --> 00:00:27.521
The Atacama Desert is one of the driest places on Earth, and one of the places most similar to Mars.

5
00:00:27.522 --> 00:00:33.141
It's the perfect place for scientists to practice for future expeditions to the red planet.

6
00:00:33.142 --> 00:00:36.524
Humans have long been fascinated with Mars.

7
00:00:36.525 --> 00:00:42.129
After all, Mars is our neighbor—just one planet further from the sun than Earth.

8
00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:49.074
The red planet, named for the Roman god of war, has long been visible to the naked eye.

9
00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:52.872
But no one knew much about Mars for centuries.

10
00:00:52.873 --> 00:00:58.942
In the 1500s scientists began to learn more about the stars and the solar system.

11
00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:04.077
They were able to calculate the position of Mars and learn about its orbit.

12
00:01:04.078 --> 00:01:12.711
In 1609 Italian scientist Galileo Galilei used an early telescope for a closer view of Mars.

13
00:01:12.712 --> 00:01:18.430
As telescopes improved in the 1700s, so did the study of Mars.

14
00:01:18.431 --> 00:01:22.940
Scientists began to work out other details about the planet.

15
00:01:22.941 --> 00:01:28.824
Early measurements of its rotation and tilt were later proven to be nearly correct.

16
00:01:28.825 --> 00:01:34.016
Mars takes 24 hours and 37 minutes to complete a rotation.

17
00:01:34.017 --> 00:01:42.251
The planet has a tilt of around 25 degrees.

18
00:01:42.252 --> 00:01:47.036
Since these values are similar to Earth's, astronomers were curious.

19
00:01:47.037 --> 00:01:49.839
Could Mars be like Earth?

20
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.972
The red planet's physical features also encouraged people to believe that it was similar to Earth.

21
00:01:55.973 --> 00:02:04.091
Astronomers noticed light and dark areas on the planet's surface and believed them to be oceans and landmasses.

22
00:02:04.092 --> 00:02:12.857
In 1877 Giovanni Schiaparelli published a detailed map of Mars that included these Earth-like features.

23
00:02:12.858 --> 00:02:19.328
He also included long straight lines that he had observed cutting across Mars' landmass.

24
00:02:19.329 --> 00:02:23.779
These lines looked much too orderly to be natural features.

25
00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:29.259
Schiaparelli thought that they were waterways that had been made by intelligent life.

26
00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:35.813
His beliefs were disputed, and it was later realized that the lines were an optical illusion.

27
00:02:35.814 --> 00:02:41.882
But the idea of a complex civilization on Mars had already gripped people's imaginations.

28
00:02:41.883 --> 00:02:47.067
Schiaparelli's mistaken belief helped give the word "Martian" a new meaning.

29
00:02:47.068 --> 00:02:51.531
It had long been an adjective that meant "having to do with Mars."

30
00:02:51.532 --> 00:02:56.948
Now it came to be a noun, meaning an inhabitant of Mars. By

31
00:02:56.949 --> 00:03:03.177
the end of the nineteenth century, novels and stories about Martians were popular. But

32
00:03:03.178 --> 00:03:06.156
their depictions weren't always positive. In

33
00:03:06.157 --> 00:03:13.556
The War of the Worlds , a novel by British author H. G. Wells, England is invaded by Martians.

34
00:03:13.557 --> 00:03:19.018
An adaptation of Wells' novel was broadcast on the radio in 1938.

35
00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:24.087
The first part of the story was told as a series of news reports.

36
00:03:24.088 --> 00:03:29.115
Many listeners panicked because they thought Martians had really invaded Earth.

37
00:03:29.116 --> 00:03:38.855
By the 1960s the widespread belief in Martians began to disappear, but Martians remained figures in popular culture.

38
00:03:38.856 --> 00:03:46.003
Rather than scary unknown invaders, they were more likely to appear as funny characters in comedies.

39
00:03:46.004 --> 00:03:49.930
What caused humans to change their minds about Martians?

40
00:03:49.931 --> 00:03:57.123
Space exploration was finally possible, and humans eagerly raced to explore space.

41
00:03:57.124 --> 00:04:04.241
The Soviet Union and the United States sent spacecraft to fly close to Mars and take photographs.

42
00:04:04.242 --> 00:04:11.283
In the 1970s, probes landed on Mars and sent back the first photographs from the surface.

43
00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:16.874
Mars was found to be cold and dry, with a surface covered in red dust.

44
00:04:16.875 --> 00:04:19.634
There were no signs of Martians.

45
00:04:19.635 --> 00:04:25.256
Since then, many unmanned missions have been sent to explore the red planet.

46
00:04:25.257 --> 00:04:34.144
There are some similarities between Mars and Earth: Mars has polar ice caps, mountains, plains, and sand dunes.

47
00:04:34.145 --> 00:04:37.339
But the environment is not very Earth-like at all.

48
00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:41.302
The planet's atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide.

49
00:04:41.303 --> 00:04:46.226
With these conditions, no form of Earth life is possible on Mars.

50
00:04:46.227 --> 00:04:52.344
However, scientists have found the minerals and chemicals necessary for life.

51
00:04:52.345 --> 00:04:56.733
There is also evidence that Mars once contained water.

52
00:04:56.734 --> 00:05:02.201
New questions began to arise: Was there once life on Mars?

53
00:05:02.202 --> 00:05:07.773
Do microscopic forms of life still live deep under the surface of Mars?

54
00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:11.962
Might we be able to build human colonies on Mars one day?

55
00:05:11.963 --> 00:05:17.319
Some people even wonder if Mars could be transformed into a planet like Earth.

56
00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:24.323
So far Mars has turned out to be less like Earth than early astronomers believed.

57
00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:28.786
But we have a long way to go before we fully understand the red planet.

58
00:05:28.787 --> 00:05:38.526
Luckily, more missions to Mars are planned, and the unique conditions of the Atacama Desert are helping scientists to prepare for these expeditions.

59
00:05:38.527 --> 00:05:49.550
Each expedition uncovers new information about Mars, but humans have barely even scratched the surface of the red planet's mysteries.

